{"t":1794887514,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"dd0672b3-2064-4bb6-9c7a-57f6b1f7b122","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-17T02:35:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 170235\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n935 PM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nHave added mention of freezing fog to the forecast tonight,\nparticularly across mountain and river valleys.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry conditions persist through the end of the week as \n  temperatures gradually increase.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions persist through the end of the week \nas temperatures gradually increase.\n\nFor tonight, stout high pressure combined with light winds and\nmainly clear skies will support ample raditional cooling\neffects. With dew points currently in the low/mid 30s and\nexpected to lower a bit overnight, temperatures will sit near\nfreezing as the ground cools. Patchy fog may develop across the\nmountain and river valleys, some of it which may turn into\nfreezing fog given temperatures in the upper 20s to low 30s.\nHave a Special Weather Statement out for this threat, especially\nalong and west of I-81.\n\nBroad high pressure will build over the central Plains in the \nupper levels, while transitioning to a surface high pressure \nover the mid-Atlantic. This ridge will influence dry conditions \nand gradually increasing temperatures through the rest of the \nweek. Plenty of sunshine each day with highs approaching the \nmiddle to upper 60s to near 70 by Friday across the region. Lows\nat night will remain in the 30s to 40s through the week. One \ncaveat to the prolonged dry conditions and warming temperatures \ncould have implications on fire weather (see Fire Weather \nsection below). \n\nLooking towards next weekend, it is still looking quite dry at\nthis point, but there are some hints at some activity in both\nbranches of the jet stream going into early next week. At this\njuncture, not seeing any significant precipitation potential.\nThe two indications of possible precipitation chances will be a\ntrough of low pressure extending northwestward from a coastal\nlow near the Carolina Coast and an approaching cold front from\nthe Ohio Valley. As of now, there are only slight chances to\nchances of rain showers Saturday night into Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /02Z TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nMainly dry for all TAF sites through the upcoming week.\nVFR conditions are anticipated through the end of the week as \nhigh pressure remains in control. Skies should remain mostly \nclear, with winds staying below 10 knots generally. Winds \ngenerally flow WSW before shifting northwesterly Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNorthwest winds will continue to diminish and should become\nlight and variable or calm at times through much of the week.\nWinds are expected to shift southerly by Thursday night before\nshifting back to the north on Friday, generally staying below 10\nknots.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHigh pressure gradually builds overhead into the middle of the \nweek, then temperatures will begin to warm mid to late week. \nDuring this timeframe, while winds will diminish, RH values \nwill go down substantially. The dry air really builds in \nstarting Tuesday into Tuesday night, and looks to continue for \nseveral days thereafter. Overnight recoveries, especially on the\nridges, will likely be very poor as well starting Tuesday \nnight. Expect afternoon RH values to drop well into the 20s each\nday. This will also be paired with seasonably warm temperatures,\nso this will really act to dry out already very dry fuels.\n\nNot helping matters is that there is no precipitation expected \neast of the Appalachians likely through at least the first half\nof next weekend. Given the setup, will really be watching for \nany potential dry frontal passages in the long range as the next\ndays to really watch out for. There is a non-zero chance that \nsome rain could come into the forecast early next week, but \nthere is very little model agreement at this point, and it seems\njust as likely that we could see another dry frontal passage in\nthis timeframe. So, just continue to monitor the latest \nforecast over the next several days as models hopefully come \ninto better agreement.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/KLW/CJL/SRT\nAVIATION...KLW/CJL/SRT\nMARINE...KLW/CJL/SRT\nFIRE WEATHER...CJL\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794887458,"days":{"2026-11-16":{"hi":null,"lo":36,"pop":0,"short":"Clear"},"2026-11-17":{"hi":61,"lo":38,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-18":{"hi":65,"lo":39,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-19":{"hi":67,"lo":41,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-20":{"hi":68,"lo":43,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-21":{"hi":65,"lo":47,"pop":17,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-22":{"hi":66,"lo":49,"pop":28,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-23":{"hi":61,"lo":null,"pop":10,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1794884400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,9.9,9.9,9.8,9.6,9.2,8.8,8.6,8.9,9.5,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.8,9.5,9.3,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[2,2,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,4,4,3,3,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,1,2,2,2,2,2,3,3,3,2,2,2,2,2,2,1],"gst":[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,6,6,6,5,5,5,4,4,4,5,5,5,5,5,6,6,6,6,5,5,4,4,5,5,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],"dir":[330,320,320,320,320,330,320,320,320,310,310,320,330,320,320,320,320,310,300,290,280,270,260,270,280,280,280,2